# Events App — Agent Context

## What this is

A React Native (Expo) app for sharing events between people. No feeds — just a calendar of events shared person-to-person. Sharing triggers person-to-person notifications: push for recipients who have the app, SMS for those who don't.

Read `docs/events-technical-architecture.md` for the full architecture, data model, and flows. That document is the source of truth for how the app should behave.

## Discuss first, implement on approval (owner rule, 2026-08-31)

When the owner asks for help thinking through an idea — "help me think about…", "what do you think of…", "how could we…", "I'm wondering whether…", or floats a half-formed feature — that is a request for **discussion, not implementation**. The owner has been burned by agents running off and building something he never fully specified. In discussion mode:

- **Touch nothing.** No code edits, no scaffolding, no "quick prototype to show what I mean," no branches, commits, or pushes. The repo stays exactly as it was.
- **Engage like a collaborator, not a ticket-taker.** Ask the questions a good one would ask: who is it for, what problem does it solve, what happens today, what is the simplest version that works. Say what you would need to know before building anything.
- **Offer options with honest trade-offs.** Sketch two or three approaches — complexity, risk to existing behavior, fit with the architectural rules in this file — and say which you would pick and why. Push back when the idea conflicts with the design language, the data model, or a prior owner decision; that pushback is the job, not insubordination.
- **End with a recommendation or an open question, then stop.** Wait for an explicit go-ahead ("build it", "go with option 2", "implement that") before touching the repo. Enthusiasm about one approach in the discussion is approval of that approach only — not license to expand the scope.

A direct imperative ("add…", "fix…", "implement…", "ship…") is an implementation request — proceed normally. When a message is genuinely ambiguous between the two, ask one short question instead of guessing your way into code.

## Stack

- **Frontend:** React Native, Expo SDK 54, Expo Router v6 (file-based routing), TypeScript
- **Backend:** Supabase — Postgres, Auth (phone OTP), Edge Functions (Deno), RLS
- **Key libraries:** react-native-calendars, libphonenumber-js, @react-native-async-storage/async-storage, expo-notifications

## Key architectural rules

- **Every calendar entry is the owner's own row (Copy + Follow, cutover 2026-08-24 — `docs/per-user-events-copy-follow-spec.md`).** `events` rows are owner-scoped (`owner_id`); RLS is owner-only for SELECT/DELETE, and there are no client INSERT/UPDATE policies — creates and edits go through the `save_event` RPC (idempotent on the client-generated id) so the frozen/cascade logic cannot be bypassed. A field-changing save sets `frozen` and silently cascades the new values to every row still following it (no push/SMS on edit — owner decision 2026-08-21); a no-op save changes nothing and never ends following.
- **Sharing is forwarding.** The `share_event` RPC copies the caller's current row onto each recipient's calendar (`from_event_id` = the caller's row, `from_user_id` = the caller) — contacts without accounts get theirs on sign-up via the `deliver_pending_shares` trigger, stamped from the sender's row as it is at that moment. A share is a completed action — there is no unshare, and removing your row never affects anyone else's calendar (followers keep their field values; `from_event_id` goes SET NULL). `sends` is the share record (✓ Shared / delivery status, "Shared with", notifications, pending delivery), not the visibility mechanism.
- **Every send carries a yes/no response slot (Who's Coming, shipped 2026-08-28 — `FEATURES.md` → Who's Coming).** The share already is the ask; `sends.response` is the recipient's answer to the person who sent it (NULL = unanswered, no maybe, last write wins). Only the asker reads it ("Shared with"); a forward is a new ask answered to the forwarder. Recipients write it via the `respond_to_send` RPC (their own row id in; writes only on change and returns the changed flag) or from the receipt page linked on the share SMS — both SMS variants carry the link, so answering never requires the app. The asker gets a push only when an answer *changes* (`send-response-notification`, freshness-gated on `responded_at`; push only, never SMS; skipped when the asker hid the responder). The SMS line exists only while the `RESPONSE_LINK_BASE_URL` function secret is set — unsetting strips it without touching the in-app path. The receipt host is a dedicated one-page Pages project (`receipt/` → `events-reply.pages.dev`, `npm run deploy:receipt`), never the web app; its API is the `send-response` edge function (`--no-verify-jwt`, the per-send `response_token` is the credential, GET inert / POST writes). Removing a copy never changes the answer; answers die with the asker's event (sends cascade).
- **Sharing is person-to-person.** Circles are a UI shortcut — at the data level, `sends` always reference individual `my_people` rows.
- **No forced onboarding.** Users land on the calendar immediately after sign-up; someone who was shared an event sees it right away. The walkthrough (`app/(app)/onboarding.tsx`) auto-shows at most once and only when the user has no events at all; it can be reopened anytime via the `?` button in the calendar header. Contacts permission is only requested when they tap Share or navigate to My People. Sign-in itself carries a short orientation (what the app is + why the phone number) on the existing phone screen — not a separate gate.
- **No global dedup.** Two people adding "Lunch" at the same slot are two independent rows (KI-002 died with the old dedup index). Create/edit both go through `save_event` with a client-generated id. The one surviving dedup is per-user: pasting a URL already on *my* calendar in add-event offers to jump to the existing row.
- **The native app is the product; the web build is the dev/staging/CI surface** (decision 2026-08-09 — `docs/distribution-strategy.md`). The same Expo codebase runs in the browser via react-native-web, hosted at **https://shared-events.pages.dev** (Cloudflare Pages; Wrangler project name `shared-events` — `events-app.pages.dev` was globally taken), staging preview at **https://staging.shared-events.pages.dev**. All automated testing targets web, but no user flow may direct anyone to the web app — it exists for development, and the privacy policy page (`public/privacy.html` → `/privacy.html`). Web gaps are handled explicitly: manual "Add person" (name + phone) on the People screen (no contacts API on web), and HTML `date`/`time` inputs in the add/edit event forms (the native datetimepicker never opens on web). Never request browser notification permission — web users get SMS. Deploy with `npm run deploy:web` / `npm run deploy:staging` (see AGENTS.md). Beta distribution to users is native-only: TestFlight internal + Play internal testing via EAS (runbook: `docs/development-workflow.md` → Native builds).
- **RLS enforces access.** Every table has Row-Level Security. Users can only see their own data or data shared with them. Never use `USING (true)` on events.
- **Pull, not push.** The app fetches fresh data on screen focus. No real-time subscriptions. Notifications are person-triggered only (a share action calls `send-notification`) — never broadcasts or marketing pushes.
- **The OS notification prompt is explainer-gated.** `requestPermissionsAsync` has exactly one call site: the notification explainer's Continue (`NotificationPermissionGate`, shown once on the first native launch after the calendar settles, never stacked on the walkthrough). Not now persists (`notification_explainer_answered` in AsyncStorage) and never re-asks; a denied OS prompt gets no recovery screen (SMS covers it). Launch only registers the push token when permission is already granted; web never prompts.
- **Delete means remove.** Removing an event deletes the caller's own `events` row (its `sends` records cascade). Every row has exactly one owner, so there is no orphan GC — `cleanup-events`/`cleanup_old_events` died with the old model. Followers' rows survive with `from_event_id` SET NULL.
- **Don't withhold to drive opens — and don't lure either.** SMS messages include the event's own URL so recipients can act without installing the app, but carry no app/web links at all (2026-08-09): the SMS is a pure notification, not an acquisition channel. One exception during internal testing (2026-08-17): the SMS to recipients without an account ends with a signup invite (`Want to invite your friends to things too? Email kilani.ramsey@gmail.com to get signed up.`) so an interested recipient can ask the owner for beta access — an email CTA, still no links. At launch the two URLs return together (`FEATURES.md` → SMS Links at Launch): store link for non-users, event deep link for app users — same `send-notification` change, not before listings exist, never one variant without the other.
- **Themes are named moods, not OS light/dark.** Paper (default) and Evening are user-selected via the theme swatch button in the calendar header (previews the next theme's colors; cycles the registry) and persisted to AsyncStorage (`theme_preference`). Every color/type decision comes from a role token in `constants/Colors.ts` via `useTheme` — never hard-code hex values, and never read the OS color scheme. The accent is spent only on the selected day, event dots, the primary action, and the "From X" attribution; destructive is always red. `docs/events-design-language.md` is the source of truth. On web, `applyWebBrowserChrome` keeps the browser chrome (iOS Safari status bar / Dynamic Island tint, `theme-color`, `color-scheme`, `html`/`body` background) in sync with the active theme — react-native-web's `StatusBar` is a no-op.

