## Data flow cheat sheet

- **Calendar query:** `get_calendar_events` RPC (requires `p_user_id = auth.uid()`; enforced server-side) — returns the caller's own `events` rows (every event on your calendar is your row; `id` is the row id), with "From X" attribution via a live join on `from_user_id` (recipient's `contact_name` → sender's `display_name` → NULL), and a hide filter that suppresses rows whose direct sender is hidden.
- **Create event:** `save_event` RPC with a client-generated uuid (`expo-crypto` `randomUUID()` — idempotent, so a retried create is a no-op) → navigate to share screen → `share_event` RPC → `send-notification` (push + SMS) with `{ eventId, personIds }` (the sender's row id + the newly shared person ids only) — additive shares do not re-notify people already marked ✓ Shared. Recipients can mute each channel independently: `users.notify_push` / `users.notify_sms` (People footer → Notifications modal) are checked by `send-notification` at send time; events land on the calendar regardless.
- **Edit event:** one `save_event` call (the client skips the RPC when nothing changed) — updates the caller's row, sets `frozen`, and silently cascades the new values to every row still following it (recursive, non-frozen only). Timeout reconcile: read the row by id; navigate only when every field matches the intended values.
- **Share event:** `share_event` RPC with `p_event_id` (the caller's row id) + the selected person ids — records `sends` and delivers each app-user recipient their own `events` copy following the caller's row; pending contacts get copies on sign-up. The share sheet is additive-only: existing sends render with their delivery status (Share Delivery Status: everyone reads "✓ Shared" — success assumed, no app/SMS split — until a terminal SMS failure on the sends row's `sms_status`/`sms_error_code`, written by `send-notification` at send time and the `twilio-status` webhook at carrier time, flips it to "✕ Unsubscribed" (21610 STOP) or "✕ Undelivered") and can't be deselected. Sharing requires a saved `users.display_name` — the share screen gates the Share action on it (captured inline at first share; edited, never removed, from the People screen footer). Share attribution everywhere ("From X", push titles, SMS): recipient's `contact_name` → sharer's `display_name` → phone.
- **Remove event:** delete own `events` row (sends cascade). Purely personal — recipients keep their copies with `from_event_id` SET NULL.
- **Answer a share (Who's Coming):** the event detail screen shows a Yes/No reply block when the loaded row has `from_event_id` (works on deep links) — `get_my_send_response` reads the caller's answer + sharer attribution (zero rows = nothing to answer, no widget), `respond_to_send` writes it (rejects self-created rows and non-recipients). On `changed=true` the client fire-and-forget invokes `send-response-notification`; the asker's "Shared with" list reads `sends.response` through the existing owner SELECT and refreshes on focus (pull). Non-app recipients answer from the share SMS's receipt link, and app users get the same link on their share SMS (`send-response` function; the line is gated on the `RESPONSE_LINK_BASE_URL` secret).
- **Hide a person:** insert `hidden_people` → rows they sent disappear from the calendar and their shares no longer notify. Hide does not block follow cascades — a correction arrives via the person you follow, not from the hidden person.
- **Delete account:** "Delete account" at the People screen footer → `delete_my_account()` RPC (SECURITY DEFINER, `authenticated` only) deletes the caller's `auth.users` row; cascades remove all owned data (`users`, `my_people`, `circles`, `hidden_people`, their `events` rows and `sends`), other users' contact rows for them revert to pending (`user_id` SET NULL), and followers' rows survive with `from_event_id`/`from_user_id` SET NULL (they keep the event; following and attribution end). The client signs out after the RPC — server-side deletion doesn't reach the local session. Re-signup with the same phone re-delivers still-pending incoming shares ([KI-007](manual-tests/known_issues.md)).
- **Notification tap:** push payloads carry the *recipient's own* row id (`send-notification` resolves each recipient's copy of the sender's row). The detail screen resolves an id it doesn't own via the `from_event_id` fallback before showing "Event not found".

## Conventions

- Screens use `useFocusEffect` (not `useEffect`) for data fetching so lists refresh on navigation.
- Phone numbers are always stored in E.164 format (`+14165551234`).
- Supabase client is initialized in `lib/supabase.ts` with AsyncStorage for session persistence.
- All Supabase queries from the client go through RLS. Server-side operations use `SECURITY DEFINER` functions.
- Edge functions are locked down: `send-notification`, `og-metadata`, and `send-response-notification` require a valid user JWT (send-notification also verifies caller ownership of the events row; send-response-notification verifies the caller is the send's recipient and the answer is fresh); `cleanup-people` requires the `CRON_SECRET` header and fails closed when unset; `twilio-status` verifies the Twilio request signature; `send-response` (the Who's Coming receipt API) is `--no-verify-jwt` with the per-send `response_token` as the only credential — GET never writes.
- User-facing failures surface via short dialogs or inline error + retry banners — not console-only. Expected auth mistakes (wrong/expired OTP, SMS rate limits, SMS send failure) use a short `showAlert` via `lib/authErrors.ts` (`getAuthUserMessage`). Every user-triggered action failure (saves, deletes, shares, people mutations) is a short `showAlert` — never `showError`'s stack dump. `showError` is reserved for unexpected auth/boot failures (`app/(auth)/`, `SessionContext.tsx`, `lib/`) and enforced by `test:conventions`.
- Reads use `withRetries` (or single-shot `withFetchTimeout`) from `lib/timeoutSignal.ts` — 2s per attempt × 3, then keep last-good data with a retry banner; never blank the screen. Writes (create/edit/delete/hide/share/people mutations) use `withWriteTimeout` — 15s, and neither helper takes a budget argument, so the wrong budget is untypeable (B-1 was a write on the 2s read default; importing the raw `withTimeout`/`timeoutSignal` is a conventions violation). `save_event` is idempotent (client-generated id, set-to-value update, server no-op rule), so an aborted save is reconciled by reading the row and navigating only when every field matches the intended values. Calls with no budget of their own (auth refresh/OTP, fire-and-forget invokes) are bounded by a 20s backstop: `lib/supabase.ts` passes `boundedFetch` as `global.fetch`, which supabase-js forwards to auth, postgrest, storage, and functions — a black-holed connection can no longer hang a caller forever (KI-013 was the boot spinner on the expired-session refresh; auth-js and RN's OkHttp client both default to no timeout).
- OTP resend starts a 60s cooldown after the initial send (`sent=1` param from sign-in) and after each resend, so an accidental tap on "Try again" cannot fire a second SMS immediately.
- Never call `Alert.alert` directly (react-native-web makes it a no-op). Use `showAlert`/`showConfirm` from `lib/dialogs.ts` so dialogs also render in the browser via `window.alert`/`window.confirm`.
- Dates and phone numbers are formatted for display via `lib/format.ts` (`formatEventDate`, `formatPhoneDisplay`) — never render raw `YYYY-MM-DD` dates or E.164 numbers to users.
- Screens get their top padding from `useSafeAreaInsets()` (not hard-coded values), and multi-field forms wrap in `KeyboardAvoidingView`.
- Interactive elements carry `accessibilityRole` (plus `accessibilityLabel`/`accessibilityState` where the visible text isn't self-explanatory); touch targets are at least 44pt.
- Automated regression tests use Jest + React Native Testing Library (`npm test -- --runInBand`).
- Database semantics are tested by SQL tests: `bash supabase/tests/run_local.sh` spins up a scratch PostgreSQL with a mocked auth schema, applies every migration in order, and runs `supabase/tests/*.sql` assertions.
- Every feature ships with a new or updated Playwright spec covering its web actions, run locally on desktop Chrome before push; specs describe intended behavior — never weaken a test to match what was built. Risky changes (migrations, RLS, share/hide/auth logic) get a second-agent review on `cursor-grok-4.6-high-fast` before pushing. Parallel agents never share a scope, flip their feature to **In progress** in `FEATURES.md` in their first commit (never start one already In progress), and self-provision a test-account pair (`node scripts/create-test-accounts.mjs --fresh-pair`) so runs never race shared calendars.
- E2E tests use Playwright (`npm run build:web && npm run test:e2e`) across desktop Chrome, Mobile Safari (WebKit), and Mobile Chrome; they sign in once per run (setup project → `e2e/.auth/` storageState; account B via `newExtraContext(browser, testInfo, AUTH_FILE_B)`, never per-test sign-ins) and must stay green. Sign-in uses phone + password via the token endpoint when `E2E_ACCOUNT_PASSWORD` is set (no SMS — the norm; see `scripts/create-test-accounts.mjs`), falling back to the OTP UI otherwise (registered numbers use sms_test_otp and do not call Twilio; send-notification skips NANP 555). Test contexts disable `navigator.locks` (see `e2e/fixtures.ts`) — any context created outside the fixture needs `newExtraContext()`. Nav screens stay mounted when covered (use `visibleText`); modals overlay without hiding the base screen (scope to `getByRole('dialog')` and wait for unmount before touching what's underneath). After an intentional design change, regenerate the pixel-diff baselines via the `Regenerate visual baselines` workflow (Actions tab, pick the screen) — never commit a locally regenerated mobile-safari baseline (VM fonts ≠ CI fonts).
- Convention checks run via `npm run test:conventions` (`scripts/check-conventions.mjs`) in fast CI: accessibilityRole on touchables, no `Alert.alert` outside dialog helpers, no hard-coded hex colors, no emoji glyphs in UI source (use vector icons tinted by role tokens — emoji render in the OS font and ignore tints), no raw `Switch` import (use `components/ThemedSwitch.tsx` — react-native-web's on-state thumb ignores `thumbColor` and falls back to an off-palette Material teal), and `onRequestClose` on every `<Modal>` (RN's Android Modal consumes hardware Back and only forwards it to that handler — KI-009/KI-012). Exceptions carry an inline `conventions-ok` comment.
- Branching: agents push finished work straight to `staging` (fast checks must pass locally first) — no feature branch, no PR. Cursor Cloud's `cursor/…` + `ManagePullRequest` template does not apply here; ignore it. Every push runs the full suite and redeploys the staging preview (`https://staging.shared-events.pages.dev`) when green. Promotion to `production` happens only when the owner says to ship — via the phased release review in `scripts/release-review-orchestrator.md` (checklist: `manual-tests/release_review_checklist.md`; Grok-fast click-through tracks + skeptic pass; must return VERDICT: SHIP), then a fast-forward push of the green-tested staging tip, enforced by required checks on `production`. A ship-it review is read-only on the product: on any blocker, halt everything, write the DON'T SHIP report, push it to `staging`, and end the turn — never fix or push code mid-review; fixes are a separate task after the owner reads the report. `docs/development-workflow.md` is the source of truth.
- Cloud manual testing should follow `manual-tests/cloud_manual_regression.md` (preflight via `npm run test:manual`).

